#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069964 :tutorial/content "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation $2x^2 +8 = 0$ for $x.$ First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\[3pt]\n\t\t2x^2 &= -8 \\[3pt]\n\t\tx^2 &= -4\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5649dc90-3e67-5125-9612-6e0618aea772" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
